  <!-- ========== 3. 24-72H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24–72h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">

      <p>The Israel-Lebanon ceasefire framework held and extended. US State Dept spokesperson Tommy Pigott announced May 15 that Israel and Lebanon agreed to a 45-day extension following Washington talks described as "highly productive." Both parties acknowledged the extension; a Pentagon-facilitated mil-to-mil coordination track starts May 29, with the next political talks set for June 2-3 in Washington. Israeli strikes near Tyre (southern Lebanon, May 16) — killing at least six people — show the gap between the political framework and ground-level operations, but the strikes remain outside Greater Beirut governorate and do not void the formal extension. → moved <span class="cite">N2</span></p>

      <p>The US-Iran diplomatic track entered a documented deadlock without formal closure. Araghchi at the BRICS foreign ministers' meeting in New Delhi (May 15) described the enrichment question as "almost in a deadlock," with both sides agreeing to "postpone" that issue to later negotiation stages. Araghchi simultaneously expressed "doubt about US seriousness" while keeping the door conditional: "the moment we feel they are serious… we will certainly proceed." Trump sent a new nuclear proposal to Iran on May 16 — the talks have not ended, but neither the memo-signing horizon (F2) nor the formal rejection horizon (F3) resolved YES; both closed NO, encoding the ambiguous middle state as a structural feature of the board. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait restored US military basing access earlier than the inherited forecast board had captured. Wall Street Journal reporting from May 7, corroborated by IranIntl and TheDefenseNews, confirmed that Saudi Arabia and Kuwait lifted restrictions on US military access to their bases and airspace following a second Trump-MBS phone call. The subsequent Trump Riyadh visit of May 13-14 — which produced $142 billion in Saudi arms agreements conducted from the restored basing framework — constitutes named-principal confirmation satisfying the resolution criteria. The 18-day gap between the underlying event (May 7) and the board's horizon date (May 25) represents the largest cold-start calibration failure in this session: p_inherited at 0.15 against a p_cold of 0.72, a 57pp gap, acknowledged as a prior-version undercount. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>The Israeli domestic political crisis reached a formal threshold that had been undetected by the forecast board since its May 11 seeding. Sunday audit confirmed Degel HaTorah and Agudat Yisrael formally departed the Netanyahu government in July 2025, reducing the coalition to 60 seats — below the 61-seat majority specified in F7's resolution criteria — per NPR, i24NEWS, and the official record on the 37th Government. By May 2026, Degel HaTorah stated publicly "we have no trust in Netanyahu anymore" and filed dissolution bills alongside opposition parties; Speaker Ohana set the earliest preliminary reading for May 20, concentrating the dissolution cascade across three active forecasts. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span>, <span class="cite">N1</span></p>

      <p>Commercial shipping through the Strait of Hormuz remained at historical lows through mid-May, with no material change in the underlying conditions. USNI News (May 1) documented Hormuz transits at their lowest level since Operation Epic Fury commenced February 28. Lloyd's Market Association confirmed war-risk premiums in the 3-8% range (against 0.25% pre-war), with LMA noting that safety conditions — not insurance unavailability — constitute the operative deterrent for Western carriers. Project Freedom launched May 3-4 and paused May 5 following Trump's "mutual agreement" announcement with Iran; as of May 17 no formal restart has occurred. The Saudi-Kuwait airspace restoration removes one logistical barrier but does not supply the diplomatic clearance from Tehran that a Project Freedom restart requires. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">N6</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:18%;">Escalation 18%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:57%;">Protracted 57%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:25%;">De-esc 25%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (18%):</strong> Primarily driven by N6 (Project Freedom restart, 28%) — a restart would create conditions for Iranian kinetic response; weighted by estimated 65% probability of kinetic response to a resumed escort operation under current diplomatic conditions.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (57%):</strong> The dominant scenario: talks alive but frozen (N4 at coin-flip 55% for a facilitated meeting), Israeli elections underway (N1/N3 cascading), Hormuz sealed (F6 at floor 5%), Lebanon ceasefire holding — the structural deadlock state with no near-term resolution pathway on the Iran track.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (25%):</strong> Anchored by N4 (55%) and N7 (72%) — if a US-Iran face-to-face meeting occurs before May 31 AND Khamenei breaks his 79-day public silence on the negotiation track before June 7, the combined signal weight (joint probability ~0.40, discounted to ~0.25 for the gap between meeting and actual de-escalation) would shift the board toward a de-escalatory trajectory; Lebanon ceasefire extension (N2 RESOLVED-YES) provides the structural framework for that outcome.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>

      <p>Iran's state media apparatus maintained its dual-register approach through mid-May: projecting resolve for the domestic audience while preserving diplomatic optionality for the external one. Press TV and IRNA covered Araghchi's BRICS appearance extensively, centering the "never wanted nuclear weapons" framing and presenting the mutual decision to postpone the enrichment question as a diplomatic achievement rather than a concession. Araghchi's "doubt about US seriousness" received prominent IRNA placement — a calibrated signal to the domestic audience watching war erode institutional confidence in external partners. Baghaei's restatement of war-end prerequisites drew standard coverage notable for its repetition rather than any new content. → supports <span class="cite">N4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Hardliner outlets pushed further along the domestically-oriented flank. Mohsen Rezaei's characterization of any Hormuz deal as "unrealistic without reparations for war damage" circulated in Kayhan and Fars News, maintaining the pressure track that constrains Araghchi's negotiating room. The Lebanon ceasefire extension received minimal Iranian state media attention — the 45-day Hochstein deal is treated as a bilateral Israeli-Lebanese matter tangential to Tehran's core strategic concerns. Project Freedom's May 5 pause continued to receive triumphal framing in state media archival coverage, without substantive update on the current diplomatic status of the Hormuz file. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The signal that concentrates forecast attention: no Khamenei statement has directly addressed the negotiation track since the April 8 ceasefire — 79 war-days of public silence from the Supreme Leader on the file that most directly conditions Iran's strategic posture. This silence could reflect deliberate ambiguity, internal deliberation not yet resolved, or a decision to let Araghchi carry the public channel while the real decision-making occurs at a higher level. Each of these interpretations is consistent with the observable evidence; none can be distinguished in open sources without a statement from Khamenei himself. → supports <span class="cite">N7</span> without move</p>

    </div>
  </div>

